#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A break record that quotes an anchor must still be able to find it.

MISE description="Every quoted break anchor resolves exactly once in its target"
"""
# This exists because three records on `main` quoted anchors that no longer
# resolved, and **not one of them was red**. `scripts/break-once.sh` refuses to
# run when its search literal is not exactly-once in the target, so a rotted
# anchor makes the recorded run unreproducible — while the record goes on
# reading like evidence. That is the failure this task turns into a gate.
#
# The rule it enforces is `break-once.sh`'s own precondition, nothing more:
#
#     grep -qF -- "$from" "$file"          # count 0 aborts before any backup
#     assert text.count(old) == 1          # count 2 aborts, backup restored
#
# So the check is `count == 1`, and both failure modes are safe to hit by hand:
# a 0 aborts at the grep before a backup exists, and a 2 aborts at the python
# assert with the backup already restored by `trap EXIT`.
#
# ── three ways a quoted anchor rots, all three found on one branch ───────────
#
# 1. **Same file.** A record in the file it breaks makes its own anchor a second
#    occurrence: count 2. Quoting can never work there, so those records
#    describe the search edit instead — and still quote the REPLACEMENT, which
#    is always safe because `break-once.sh` never searches `$to`.
# 2. **The formatter rewrapped it.** A record quoted a one-line expression;
#    `rustfmt` split it across three when the function's signature widened.
#    Count 0, cross-file, nothing anybody wrote by hand. The formatter owns the
#    line breaks and the record does not.
# 3. **A positional anchor.** A record anchored on a slice of `lib.rs`'s
#    `pub use` list. A later wave inserted one more export into the middle of
#    that slice: count 0. Cross-file and nothing rewrapped, so neither rule
#    above reaches it. What moved was a sibling's presence in an ordered list.
# 4. **A fragment, or a line whose text prefixes another.** The standard advice
#    is "pass the line WITH its indentation", and for these it does not work:
#    a `const` at column 0 has no indentation to add; a mid-line fragment like
#    `pop_front()` is not disambiguated by indentation at all; and a line whose
#    indented text is a PREFIX of another line still counts 2 unless the anchor
#    carries the trailing NEWLINE, which no record thinks to mention. So an
#    anchor must be a **whole line, including indentation, plus the newline when
#    its text prefixes another line.** The abort reads as the harness failing
#    when it is the record that is wrong.
#
# The question that reaches all four: **is the anchor's uniqueness a property of
# the LINE, or of its NEIGHBOURS?** — with the sharpening mode 4 forces, that a
# fragment is not a line at all.
#
# ── why this list is written out by hand ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Deriving it would mean parsing prose for "replacing `X` with `Y` in `path`",
# and a heuristic that misses a record checks nothing while reporting success.
# So the list is explicit, exactly as `local.rs`'s
# `no_failure_prefix_is_a_prefix_of_another` spells out its four prefixes and
# says a fifth has to be added by hand.
#
# **The honest limitation, and it is larger than it sounds: this checks the
# records REGISTERED below, not the records that exist.** A sweep of every
# backticked code-shaped span inside a break record, repo-wide, finds them in the
# hundreds. Most are watched output or replacement literals rather than search
# anchors — which is exactly why no extractor can build this list — but the
# consequence stands: a green here means *the registered anchors resolve*, and it
# is not a statement about the health of every record in the tree.
#
# Registration is therefore **opt-in per record**, and the green line says
# "registered" for that reason. When you write a record that quotes a search
# literal, add it. Nothing will notice if you do not, which is the one hole this
# task cannot close from the inside.
#
# **And it checks ANCHORS, never MESSAGES.** A record's watched output is a claim
# about which assertion fires first, and that rots when an assertion is added
# ABOVE the recorded one — the guard stays correct, the test still fails, and only
# comparing the recorded sentence against a fresh run finds it. No count and no
# resolution check can see that. `scripts/replay-fate-breaks.sh` is the tool for
# it, on the set it covers; this task deliberately answers the narrower question
# of whether the break can be STARTED at all.
import pathlib
import subprocess
import sys

ROOT = pathlib.Path(
    subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"],
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=True,
    ).stdout.strip()
)

# (record id, where the record lives, file the break edits, quoted SEARCH literal)
#
# Only records that QUOTE a search literal belong here. A record that describes
# its edit has nothing to resolve and nothing to rot — see the three modes above
# for when describing is the right call.
ANCHORS = [
    ("R15", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/idempotency.rs",
     "Self::Only(named) => named.contains(&method),"),
    ("R16", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/idempotency.rs",
     "Self::Only(named) => named.contains(&method),"),
    ("R17", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "return Claimed::None;"),
    ("R18", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "Some(_) | None => KeyState::Fresh,"),
    ("R20", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "keylink/src/idempotency.rs",
     "self.ledger.settle(self.peer, &self.key, slot);"),
    ("R25", "keylink/tests/api.rs", "keylink/src/macros.rs",
     "pub type SystemBuilder = $crate::SystemBuilder<Mail>;"),
    ("R26b", "keylink/tests/api.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "pub fn keyable(mut self, keyable: Keyable) -> Self {"),
    ("R26c", "keylink/tests/api.rs", "keylink/src/rpc.rs",
     "pub fn records(mut self, records: Records) -> Self {"),
    ("R28", "keylink/tests/blobs.rs", "keylink/src/macros.rs",
     "__keylink_granted_id!"),
]

# Records whose search edit is DESCRIBED, deliberately, and must stay that way.
#
# **This is documentation and NOT a check, and the reason is worth stating because
# it looks checkable and is not.** The obvious assertion — "the retired literal no
# longer appears in the record's file" — fails immediately: a record that explains
# *why* it was converted quotes the retired literal to do so, and an explanatory
# quote is textually identical to a search quote. Measured, not assumed: R26a's file
# contains its retired anchor exactly once, and that once is the paragraph
# documenting the rot mode. Any rule strong enough to catch a re-quote would flag
# that paragraph, and any rule loose enough to permit it catches nothing.
#
# What the list is for, then: **distinguishing "deliberately described" from
# "forgotten".** Without it a record absent from ANCHORS is ambiguous, and the
# ambiguity favours the wrong reading — somebody re-quotes an anchor that was
# converted on purpose, reinstating the rot the conversion removed, and the new
# entry looks like coverage.
DESCRIBED = [
    ("R11", "keylink/src/idempotency.rs", "same-file",
     "Guard::drop's NotDispatched condition - the record's own quote was the "
     "second occurrence"),
    ("R19", "keylink/tests/idempotency.rs", "same-file",
     "the keyed-actor test's own .keyable(..) call, for the same reason"),
    ("R26a", "keylink/tests/api.rs", "positional",
     "a slice of lib.rs's `pub use` list - re-quoting buys only until the next "
     "export lands"),
]

def flat(text: str) -> str:
    """Whitespace-collapsed, so a literal wrapped across `///` lines still matches.

    A record's quote is broken by the comment's own wrapping; the target's copy is
    not. Comparing them raw finds nothing and reports a false failure - the same
    trap a plain `grep -c` over a record hits.
    """
    return " ".join(text.split())


cache: dict[str, str] = {}
flatcache: dict[str, str] = {}
bad = 0

for record, lives_in, target, literal in ANCHORS:
    if target not in cache:
        cache[target] = (ROOT / target).read_text()
    found = cache[target].count(literal)
    if found == 1:
        continue
    bad += 1
    if found == 0:
        reason = "IS GONE - `break-once.sh` aborts at its grep, before any backup"
        likely = "the line was edited, or the formatter rewrapped it"
    else:
        reason = f"IS NOT UNIQUE ({found}x) - `break-once.sh` aborts on its count assert"
        # At least three causes, and an earlier version of this line named only the
        # first. It was wrong for three real failures at once on a sibling branch:
        # two same-file FRAGMENTS and one cross-file PREFIX collision. A tool that
        # prints the right paragraph for the wrong failure is this whole convention
        # one level up, so it enumerates instead of guessing.
        likely = (
            "one of: (a) the record itself now quotes it - the same-file trap; "
            "(b) the anchor is a FRAGMENT, not a whole line, and no amount of "
            "indentation makes a fragment unique; (c) the anchor is a whole line "
            "whose text PREFIXES another line, which needs the trailing newline "
            "too. Check which before editing - the remedy differs"
        )
    print(f"FAIL {record}: the anchor {reason}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"       likely cause:    {likely}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"       record lives in: {lives_in}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"       break edits:     {target}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"       anchor:          {literal}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)

# ── and is it the literal the RECORD actually quotes? ─────────────────────────
#
# Without this the task is the very defect it exists to catch, one level up: it
# hardcodes a COPY of a value that lives in a doc comment in another file, so a
# rebase that correctly re-quotes a record and forgets this list leaves the gate
# validating a string no record uses - green.
#
# `local.rs`'s `no_failure_prefix_is_a_prefix_of_another` is NOT the precedent for
# a hand-written list of copies. Its own comment says it "names the consts rather
# than the strings, so it cannot drift from the wire" - hand-written *references*
# to one source of truth. A doc comment cannot be referenced from Python, so the
# copy is unavoidable here; what was avoidable is leaving it unchecked.
for record, lives_in, target, literal in ANCHORS:
    if lives_in not in flatcache:
        flatcache[lives_in] = flat((ROOT / lives_in).read_text())
    if flat(literal) in flatcache[lives_in]:
        continue
    bad += 1
    print(f"FAIL {record}: this task's literal is NOT QUOTED by its own record",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"       record lives in: {lives_in}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"       task's literal:  {literal}", file=sys.stderr)
    print("       The record was re-quoted and this list was not, or the row names",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       the wrong record. The RECORD is the source of truth; follow it.",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)

# ── population tripwire ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A count, deliberately, and the safe kind: it can only fail LOUDLY. There is no
# state in which it passes while wrong, which is what separates it from the
# whole-target pass/fail counts this convention retired. The rule is not "never
# write a count" - it is "never write a count that can pass while wrong".
#
# What it buys: ADDING a break record forces a decision about registering it,
# instead of this list silently falling further behind a growing population.
RECORD_BLOCKS = 98

blocks = 0
for path in sorted(
    list((ROOT / "keylink").rglob("*.rs")) + list((ROOT / "keylink-persist").rglob("*.rs"))
):
    run, found = [], []
    for line in path.read_text().splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if stripped.startswith(("///", "//!", "//")):
            run.append(stripped)
        else:
            if run:
                found.append(" ".join(run))
            run = []
    if run:
        found.append(" ".join(run))
    blocks += sum(1 for b in found if "break-once.sh" in b)

if blocks != RECORD_BLOCKS:
    bad += 1
    print(f"FAIL population: {blocks} comment blocks mention break-once.sh, but "
          f"RECORD_BLOCKS says {RECORD_BLOCKS}.", file=sys.stderr)
    print("       A record was added or removed. Decide whether it needs a row",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       above - most will not, and that is fine - then update this",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("       number. This exists so the decision is forced, not skipped.",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print(file=sys.stderr)

if bad:
    # The remedy belongs HERE and not only in RFC-08 §9.2, because a contributor
    # meets the failure and not the convention. Without it the shortest path to
    # green is to delete the entry — which removes the check while reporting
    # success, the exact silent-success case this task exists to prevent.
    print(
        f"{bad} of {len(ANCHORS)} quoted break anchors no longer resolve, so those\n"
        "records cannot be replayed and nothing else would have said so.\n"
        "\n"
        "THIS MEANS THE ANCHOR MOVED. FOLLOW IT.\n"
        "\n"
        "  Re-quote it against the line as it now reads. That is the fix for a\n"
        "  cross-file, line-local anchor, which is most of this list, and it is\n"
        "  the fix whenever a rebase or a refactor moved the target.\n"
        "\n"
        "Delete the entry ONLY in the two cases where quoting cannot work:\n"
        "\n"
        "  - the record lives in the file its break edits, so its own quote is\n"
        "    the second occurrence; or\n"
        "  - the anchor's uniqueness is a property of its NEIGHBOURS rather than\n"
        "    of the line - a `pub use` list item, a slice of an ordered list -\n"
        "    where re-quoting only buys until the next sibling lands.\n"
        "\n"
        "  In both - and ONLY in those two - describe the search edit instead.\n"
        "  Quote the REPLACEMENT either way: `break-once.sh` never searches it.\n"
        "\n"
        "  AND REPLAY THE RECORD FROM ITS NEW DESCRIPTION BEFORE DROPPING THE ROW.\n"
        "  A description is ungated: nothing checks that it locates the edit. The\n"
        "  first three conversions made under this rule included one that sent a\n"
        "  reader to a line where the guard passes, which reads as `this guard is\n"
        "  vacuous` and is false. Quoting is the safe side BECAUSE of this gate.\n"
        "\n"
        "Deleting an entry is the shortest path to green and it is usually the\n"
        "wrong one: it makes the record unchecked rather than correct. If you are\n"
        "mid-rebase and reaching for it, that is the moment this paragraph is for.\n"
        "RFC-08 §9.2 carries the full argument.",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    sys.exit(1)

print(
    f"ok: {len(ANCHORS)} REGISTERED break anchors resolve exactly once "
    "(registration is per-record and opt-in - see this task's header)"
)
print(
    f"    {len(DESCRIBED)} more are recorded as deliberately DESCRIBED rather than "
    "quoted - documentation, not a check"
)
